best describes the osmolarity of this solution? (Note : Na = 23, Cl = 35.5)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum","options":[{"label":"A","text":"340 mosmol/L"},{"label":"B","text":"680 mosmol/L"},{"label":"C","text":"340 osmol/L"},{"label":"D","text":"680 osmol/L"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-279ff63e7ef7","stem":"The isotonic concentration for sodium chloride is 0.9 %, and for glucose is 5 %. Which of the following solutions is isotonic with the above solutions? A solu- tion containing","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"5 % glucose and 0.25 % sodium chloride"},{"label":"B","text":"0.9 % sodium chloride and 0.5 % glucose"},{"label":"C","text":"2.5 % sodium chloride and 2.5 % glucose"},{"label":"D","text":"4 % glucose and 0.18 % sodium chloride"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-62de86cbd815","stem":"Given that one mole of glucose has a mass of 180 g, how many millimole of glucose does 40 g of glucose contain?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.222"},{"label":"B","text":"180"},{"label":"C","text":"222"},{"label":"D","text":"278"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-115426f5960d","stem":"Which of the following statements about an “osmole” is correct? An osmole is the","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"same as a mole for ionic substances."},{"label":"B","text":"formula weight of a substance expressed in grams."},{"label":"C","text":"number of moles multiplied by the number of molecules in the chemical formula."},{"label":"D","text":"amount of substance that must be dissolved in order to produce 1.6 × 1023 dissolved particles."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-1ae44b5dde15","stem":"Glucose is a sugar with a molecular formula C6H12O6. Given that the atomic weights are: C = 12, H = 1 and O = 16, what is the mass of 1 mole of glucose?","type":"Calculation","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.8 g"},{"label":"B","text":"18 g"},{"label":"C","text":"180 g"},{"label":"D","text":"1800 g"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{"sourceStem":"Glucose is a sugar with a molecular formula C6H12O6. Given that the atomic weights are: C = 12, H = 1 and O = 16, what is the mass of 1 mole of glucose?","sourceOptions":[{"label":"A","text":"1.8 g"},{"label":"B","text":"18 g"},{"label":"C","text":"180 g"},{"label":"D","text":"1800 g Answer is C: Mass of one mole of glucose = (6 × 12) + (12 × 1) + (6 × 16) = 72 + 12 + 96 = 180 g."}]}},{"id":"q-bba2390dfb50","stem":"Lactose is a sugar with a molecular formula C12H22O11. Given that the atomic weights are: C = 12, H = 1 and O = 16, what is the weight of 1 mole of lactose?","type":"Calculation","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"3.42 g"},{"label":"B","text":"34.2 g"},{"label":"C","text":"342 g"},{"label":"D","text":"3420 g"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-cd2592c49b78","stem":"Red blood cells are added to a hypotonic solution of glucose. Which of the fol- lowing best describes what you would observe?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The cells would most likely sink to the bottom unaffected"},{"label":"B","text":"The cells would shrink due to water loss. 76 4 Chemistry for Physiology"},{"label":"C","text":"The cells would coagulate."},{"label":"D","text":"The cells would swell and burst due to intake of water"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"be943b2bfa5460eb458b4de83e85d0ac1b72630c"},{"id":"q-f9149bd9e34f","stem":"A solution that is said to be isotonic to blood has the same","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"percent concentration as blood."},{"label":"B","text":"number of moles of dissolved particles as blood."},{"label":"C","text":"number of osmoles per litre of dissolved particles as blood."},{"label":"D","text":"number of dissolved particles as blood."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-4c1dc1b09316","stem":"If the following amounts of the given substances were dissolved in water, which would result in four osmoles of dissolved particles?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 moles of C6H12O6"},{"label":"B","text":"2 moles of Na+Cl−"},{"label":"C","text":"2 moles of C12H22O11"},{"label":"D","text":"2 moles of (Na+)2SO42−"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"97782bc08dd46f54a2bbf126dc766e022220b15c"},{"id":"q-a79dc6d98061","stem":"What is the difference between “molarity” and “osmolarity”?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"molarity applies only to covalent compounds while osmolarity applies only to ionic compounds."},{"label":"B","text":"osmolarity is molarity multiplied by two."},{"label":"C","text":"the molarity and osmolarity of a solution is the same for dissolved ionic compounds, but are different for dissolved covalent compounds."},{"label":"D","text":"osmolarity refers to the concentration of dissolved particles in a solution which may not be the same as the number of moles of substance that was dissolved per litre of solution."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-0f34f3f32df8","stem":"Human blood has an osmolarity that lies within the range 280– 300 mosmol/L. Which of the following statements is correct?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"an isotonic solution has osmolarity that is either less than 280 or greater than 300 mosmol/L"},{"label":"B","text":"a hypotonic solution has osmolarity between 280 and 300 mosmol/L"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"718d25ca99d028018ddc30f9be04fc82863393de"},{"id":"q-5acac8e765b3","stem":"4 Tonicity, Moles and Osmoles 77","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"C","text":"a hypertonic solution has osmolarity between 280 and 300 mosmol/L"},{"label":"D","text":"an isotonic solution has osmolarity between 280 and 300 mosmol/L"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-da83ab130856","stem":"What distinguishes an osmole from a mole?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"In a sample of substance, the number of osmoles is twice the number of moles"},{"label":"B","text":"Both mole and osmole may be used in reference to ionic compounds, while covalent compounds are described by mole alone."},{"label":"C","text":"the mass of a mole is the sum of the relative atomic masses (RAM) of the atoms in the formula stated as grams. An osmole is half of this mass."},{"label":"D","text":"an osmole applies only to covalent molecular substances, whereas a mole is validly applied to both covalent and ionic substances."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-f86d7597be6b","stem":"Which of the four IV solutions below is hypotonic?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.18 % sodium chloride and 4 % glucose"},{"label":"B","text":"0.3 % sodium chloride and 3.3 % glucose"},{"label":"C","text":"0.45 % sodium chloride"},{"label":"D","text":"0.9 % sodium chloride"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["answer_text_in_option","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-d56470811156","stem":"Which of the following solutions is the most hypertonic?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.3 % sodium chloride and 3.3 % glucose"},{"label":"B","text":"0.45 % sodium chloride and 2.5 % glucose"},{"label":"C","text":"0.45 % sodium chloride"},{"label":"D","text":"10 % glucose"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-3f84c34079e6","stem":"Given that a mole is 6 × 1023 particles, what is a millimole?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 × 1020 particles"},{"label":"B","text":"one thousand moles"},{"label":"C","text":"6 × 1026 particles"},{"label":"D","text":"0.0001 moles"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_no_source_key_bulk_v4","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"5bccc2960fb80fda5127aff2622ff91d2f28de92"},{"id":"q-19fb1d72d145","stem":"A solution that is isotonic to blood plasma is one which","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"contains 0.5 % glucose"},{"label":"B","text":"must contain the same solutes as blood and in the same concentration as in blood"},{"label":"C","text":"has an osmolarity between 280 and 300 mosmol/L"},{"label":"D","text":"causes no net movement of water into or out of cells"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-41f2905af797","stem":"Which of the following quantities of substance would, when dissolved in one litre of water, produce a solution with the highest osmolarity?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 mole of glucose molecules (C6H12O6)"},{"label":"B","text":"1 mole of Na+Cl− (sodium chloride)"},{"label":"C","text":"1 mole of haemoglobin molecules"},{"label":"D","text":"1 osmole of K+ (potassium ions)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-ded0afeef1fb","stem":"Given the concentration of Na+ inside the cell is about 10 mmol/L while it is 140 mmol/L in the extracellular fluid. The values for K+ are 140 mmol/L inside the cell and 4 mmol/L extracellularly. In what directions would these ions diffuse?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Na+ would diffuse into the cell while K+ would diffuse out."},{"label":"B","text":"K+ would diffuse into the cell while Na + would diffuse out."},{"label":"C","text":"Both Na+ and K+ would diffuse out of the cell."},{"label":"D","text":"Both Na+ and K+ would diffuse into of the cell."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-8547f1fbb426","stem":"Which of the following statements best describes what all isotonic solutions have in common? They","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum","options":[{"label":"A","text":"contain the same concentration of glucose molecules"},{"label":"B","text":"contain the same total concentration of particles"},{"label":"C","text":"contain the same concentration of sodium chloride"},{"label":"D","text":"have the same specific gravity"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"425e24882dee90b62e4827229cc905052118427c"},{"id":"q-1eef10d2546e","stem":"Which of the following statements is CORRECT?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"An acid is an electron donor and a base is an electron acceptor."},{"label":"B","text":"An acidic solution has a pH less than 7 and a basic solution has a pH greater than 7."},{"label":"C","text":"Neutralization of a strong acid by a strong base gives only water."},{"label":"D","text":"The pH of saliva is normally in the range of 8.5–9.5"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"0d28a95bedf91d4d0aa49ac7fa44d552aa3415fb"},{"id":"q-cbe515765e62","stem":"Which statement about buffers below is most correct? 80 4 Chemistry for Physiology","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Physiology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A buffer is any acid and base which together control the concentration of pH in the blood."},{"label":"B","text":"A buffer is the solution which allows CO2 to be lost from the lungs in order to control the pH of the lungs."},{"label":"C","text":"A buffer is a weak base and its acid salt or a weak acid and its basic salt. The ratio of the two components helps maintain blood pH levels."},{"label":"D","text":"A buffer is a mixture of two acids which together help to maintain blood pH"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Physiology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"],"manualEdits":{"sourceStem":"Which statement about buffers below is most correct? 80 4 Chemistry for Physiology","sourceOptions":[{"label":"A","text":"A buffer is any acid and base which together control the concentration of pH in the blood."},{"label":"B","text":"A buffer is the solution which allows CO2 to be lost from the lungs in order to control the pH of the lungs."},{"label":"C","text":"A buffer is a weak base and its acid salt or a weak acid and its basic salt. The ratio of the two components helps maintain blood pH levels."},{"label":"D","text":"A buffer is a mixture of two acids which together help to maintain blood pH Answer is C: A buffer has two conjugate components, one dealing with acid while the other destroys base. Their ratio determines the pH of the solution they are in."}],"operations":["trailing_answer_leakage_removed"]}},{"id":"q-75f7a686dfa1","stem":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"An acid is a proton donor and a base is a proton acceptor."},{"label":"B","text":"An acidic solution has a pH greater than 7 and a basic solution has a pH less than 7."},{"label":"C","text":"Neutralization of an acid by a base gives a solution of salt in water."},{"label":"D","text":"The pH of the stomach is normally in the range of 1.6–1.8"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"ddd53843a7c3208057acb162a2c5585b42e5b16c"},{"id":"q-8f4094cd2b5e","stem":"Neutralization of an acid by a base can be represented by the following equation: H 3 O + + OH − → 2 H 2 O This means that the pH of the solution after the neutralization is approximately","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"6"},{"label":"B","text":"7"},{"label":"C","text":"8"},{"label":"D","text":"9"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["answer-leak or footer removed"]},{"id":"q-2dc92e2e86c3","stem":"A buffer solution consisting of citric acid and citrate in a ratio of 5:1 maintains the pH at 7.4. What would be the ratio of the two components if the pH were to become 6.4","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Buffer","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"6:1"},{"label":"B","text":"10:1"},{"label":"C","text":"50:1"},{"label":"D","text":"1:10"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"independently_reasoned_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"00bf77cccdeec2987aaf288c7b982063ce8164bf"},{"id":"q-4a5ea749056b","stem":"Which of the following statements is closest to a correct definition of an acid?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"a substance that ionises in a solution to produce hydronium and hydroxide ions."},{"label":"B","text":"a substance that reacts with water to produce hydroxide ions."},{"label":"C","text":"a substance that dissociates in water to produce a solution with pH greater than 7.0."},{"label":"D","text":"a substance that reacts partly with water to produce a low concentration of hydronium ions."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-14e81a85e139","stem":"The pH of a buffered solution depends on","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Ph","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the ratio of the components of the buffer solution."},{"label":"B","text":"the amount of acid added to the buffer solution."},{"label":"C","text":"the amount of base added to the buffer solution."},{"label":"D","text":"the amount of acid and of base added to the solution."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{"sourceStem":"The pH of a buffered solution depends on","sourceOptions":[{"label":"A","text":"the ratio of the components of the buffer solution."},{"label":"B","text":"the amount of acid added to the buffer solution."},{"label":"C","text":"the amount of base added to the buffer solution."},{"label":"D","text":"the amount of acid and of base added to the solution. Answer is A: The pH of the buffered solution will change very little when either acid or base is added."}],"operations":["trailing_answer_leakage_removed"]}},{"id":"q-9630d7d031ca","stem":"The long term acid-base balance in the body is controlled by","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the phosphate and carbonic acid/bicarbonate buffers in the blood"},{"label":"B","text":"the kidneys and the lungs"},{"label":"C","text":"the phosphate, carbonic acid/bicarbonate, and protein buffers in the blood and cells."},{"label":"D","text":"the kidneys"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-9fb9b91635ab","stem":"You have a bottle of a strong acid and you add 100 ml of this to one litre of water. What would the pH of this final solution most likely be?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"pH = 1"},{"label":"B","text":"pH = 5"},{"label":"C","text":"pH = 7"},{"label":"D","text":"pH = 11"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-40742efa38e7","stem":"What is the approximate range of pH of gastric juice in the stomach?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Abdomen","topicOriginal":"Abdomen","subtopic":"Stomach","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.6–1.8"},{"label":"B","text":"6.2–7.4 82 4 Chemistry for Physiology"},{"label":"C","text":"7.3–7.5"},{"label":"D","text":"7.8–8.6"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Abdomen","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"a59064fb39ad98413e8cc213d76f385f0a2336f9"},{"id":"q-0dfc3d4e0a02","stem":"A buffer solution consisting of acetic acid and acetate ions (base) in a ratio of acid to base of 1:20 maintains the pH at 7.4. What is the ratio of the two com- ponents if the pH were to become 8.4 after addition of more of the basic component.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Buffer","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"1:200"},{"label":"B","text":"1:21"},{"label":"C","text":"1:30"},{"label":"D","text":"1:40"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-37a33776a240","stem":"Acidity is stated as a pH value. If the pH of urine sample “A” is 6 and the pH of urine sample “B” is 7, then which of the following is true?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the most acidic sample is sample B."},{"label":"B","text":"sample A has 10 times the hydroxide ion concentration of sample B."},{"label":"C","text":"the B sample has 10 times the hydrogen ion concentration of sample A."},{"label":"D","text":"sample A has 10 times the hydrogen ion concentration of sample B."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-7dd4abeaee24","stem":"One form of acid base imbalance in the body is called acidosis. In this situation, which of the following is true?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the blood is less alkaline than it should be."},{"label":"B","text":"the blood’s pH is less than 7.0"},{"label":"C","text":"the blood is less acidic than it should be."},{"label":"D","text":"the concentration of hydrogen ions in the blood is less than it should be."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-bd550a761986","stem":"One of the buffer systems in the blood is the carbonic acid/bicarbonate buffer. It helps to maintain the body’s acid-base balance by destroying any excess","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Buffer","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"hydrogen ions in the blood."},{"label":"B","text":"acid or base in the blood."},{"label":"C","text":"hydroxide ions formed in the blood."},{"label":"D","text":"bicarbonate ions formed in the blood."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"0078a37ee642932c77f328898b1d18538ceab90a"},{"id":"q-ac9588515e0c","stem":"The major buffer system in the extracellular compartment is the","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Buffer","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the protein buffer"},{"label":"B","text":"carbonic acid/bicarbonate buffer"},{"label":"C","text":"ammonia buffer"},{"label":"D","text":"phosphate buffer"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-41c7b354994e","stem":"If a patient was suffering from “acidosis”, what would this mean?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"blood pH is not sufficiently alkaline."},{"label":"B","text":"blood pH is acidic."},{"label":"C","text":"there is too little hydronium ion in the plasma."},{"label":"D","text":"blood pH is too acidic."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-079deba98323","stem":"What happens when an acid is added to a buffered solution?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Acid","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the solution becomes acidic"},{"label":"B","text":"the pH of the solution decreases significantly"},{"label":"C","text":"the pH of the solution decreases very slightly"},{"label":"D","text":"the pH of the solution increases slightly"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{"sourceStem":"What happens when an acid is added to a buffered solution?","sourceOptions":[{"label":"A","text":"the solution becomes acidic"},{"label":"B","text":"the pH of the solution decreases significantly"},{"label":"C","text":"the pH of the solution decreases very slightly"},{"label":"D","text":"the pH of the solution increases slightly Answer is C: A buffer will resist change to its pH. But pH does alter very slightly as the basic component destroys the added acid, and in the process becomes its cognate partner. This changes the ratio of the two components of the buffer slightly, and hence the pH."}],"operations":["trailing_answer_leakage_removed"]}},{"id":"q-50fe2888b5b9","stem":"Which of the statements below is correct?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The dihydrogen phosphate component of the phosphate buffer releases hydrogen ions into the lungs for excretion, and in the process, reverts to monohydrogen phosphate."},{"label":"B","text":"Haemoglobin as it passes through the lungs, releases hydronium ions, which are breathed out."},{"label":"C","text":"Carbon dioxide that is dissolved in blood diffuses into the alveoli and is breathed out."},{"label":"D","text":"Hydronium ions react with bicarbonate ions to form carbonic acid which moves into the lungs for exhalation."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"23f80172dff4880397cd4b5add9855d5a8e31a47"},{"id":"q-219547aa60a1","stem":"When aspirin (an acid) is in the stomach (an acidic environment), what may be said of the aspirin molecules?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Abdomen","topicOriginal":"Abdomen","subtopic":"Stomach","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most molecules will be un-ionised and therefore able to be pass through the stomach mucosa."},{"label":"B","text":"Most molecules will be ionised and therefore able to be pass through the stomach mucosa."},{"label":"C","text":"Most molecules will be un-ionised and therefore NOT able to be pass through the stomach mucosa."},{"label":"D","text":"Aspirin will be in the form of its salt and therefore able to be pass through the stomach mucosa."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Abdomen","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-f944929b36b9","stem":"Which statement below best describes an acid solution? The pH is less than","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"7"},{"label":"D","text":"8"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["answer-leak or footer removed"]},{"id":"q-1e795b63eaa6","stem":"If a patient has blood pH that is 7.3 (which is below the healthy range of blood pH values), which of the following is a correct statement?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the patient has alkalosis"},{"label":"B","text":"the patient has excessive alkali"},{"label":"C","text":"the patient has acidosis"},{"label":"D","text":"the patient has insufficient acid"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-0a87dea7bf66","stem":"Which of the following mechanisms results in acid being excreted from the body?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"breathing out H2PO4- (dihydrogen-phosphate) from the lungs"},{"label":"B","text":"excreting HCO3- in the urine"},{"label":"C","text":"excreting NH3 (ammonia) in the urine"},{"label":"D","text":"breathing out CO2 from the lungs"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-a7b7343f29f0","stem":"If the pH of a patient’s blood is 7.4, then it can be said that the patient has","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"acidic blood"},{"label":"B","text":"alkaline blood"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"1464e13c2b5d81f8c5e31e7de76efd1f9be465d5"},{"id":"q-38e1654f4741","stem":"5 Acids, Bases and Buffers 85","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Acid","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"C","text":"neutral blood"},{"label":"D","text":"alkalosis"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-c10d2384544c","stem":"Which of the following would happen when hydrochloric acid is added to water?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the acid would react with water to from a low concentration of hydronium ions."},{"label":"B","text":"the acid would react with water to from a high concentration of hydroxide ions."},{"label":"C","text":"the acid would react with water to from a high concentration of hydronium ions."},{"label":"D","text":"the acid would react with water to from a high concentration of bicarbonate ions."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-8b92dca3e491","stem":"If the pH of the stomach contents changed from 3 to 2, which of the following is true?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Abdomen","topicOriginal":"Abdomen","subtopic":"Stomach","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"at pH 2, the concentration of hydronium ions is two thirds of the concentra- tion at pH 3."},{"label":"B","text":"at pH 3, the concentration of hydronium ions is 50 % more than the concen- tration at pH 2."},{"label":"C","text":"at pH 2, the concentration of hydronium ions is ten times the concentration at pH 3."},{"label":"D","text":"at pH 3, the concentration of hydronium ions is ten times the concentration at pH 2."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Abdomen","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-8a6ad2d59c30","stem":"The carbonic acid and bicarbonate buffer system is one if the buffers that help to maintain the blood’s pH within the healthy range by doing which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Acid","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"carbonic acid destroys excess base in the blood while bicarbonate destroys excess acid."},{"label":"B","text":"carbonic acid destroys excess acid in the blood while bicarbonate destroys excess base."},{"label":"C","text":"carbonic acid and bicarbonate destroy excess acid."},{"label":"D","text":"carbonic acid and bicarbonate destroy excess base."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"fed8cca32a7fd1f50deb87d6a51f85dd919d9f3f"},{"id":"q-1193e28d83ab","stem":"In a solution like blood that is buffered to minimise changes in pH, what deter- mines the pH of the solution?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Buffer","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the concentration of the hydronium ions"},{"label":"B","text":"the logarithm of the concentration of the hydronium ions"},{"label":"C","text":"the ratio of the concentration of hydronium ions to hydroxide ions."},{"label":"D","text":"the ratio of the concentrations of the two components of the buffer."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-17cacfb763e1","stem":"When in solution, an acidic drug also exists as its salt. What feature does the salt of a drug have that may not be shared by the acid form of the drug?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The salt of the drug does not carry an electrical charge but the acid form does."},{"label":"B","text":"The salt of the drug may be more soluble in lipid than the acid form of the drug."},{"label":"C","text":"The acid form of the drug is ionised whereas the salt is not."},{"label":"D","text":"The salt of the drug may be more soluble in water than the acid form of the drug."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{"sourceStem":"When in solution, an acidic drug also exists as its salt. What feature does the salt of a drug have that may not be shared by the acid form of the drug?","sourceOptions":[{"label":"A","text":"The salt of the drug does not carry an electrical charge but the acid form does."},{"label":"B","text":"The salt of the drug may be more soluble in lipid than the acid form of the drug."},{"label":"C","text":"The acid form of the drug is ionised whereas the salt is not."},{"label":"D","text":"The salt of the drug may be more soluble in water than the acid form of the drug. Answer is D: The salt of the drug, being ionised, is more soluble in water (a polar liquid) than is the drug molecule."}],"operations":["trailing_answer_leakage_removed"]}},{"id":"q-a40d0219b29f","stem":"Which one of the following best defines an acid?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"an aqueous solution with a pH of less than 7.0"},{"label":"B","text":"a substance that reacts with water to produce hydrogen ions"},{"label":"C","text":"a substance that reacts with water to produce hydroxide ions"},{"label":"D","text":"a substance that reacts with water to produce hydronium ions"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-d5f99ab254f1","stem":"Which one of the following acids is not normally found in the body?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"hydrochloric acid"},{"label":"B","text":"carbonic acid"},{"label":"C","text":"nucleic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"acetylsalicylic acid"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-b348e3267dea","stem":"If a sample of blood has a pH of 7.25, which of the following is true?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"it is in the healthy range"},{"label":"B","text":"it is neutral"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"450d2cc87bcc6653dcaf2ed46b4380ab15a81539"},{"id":"q-235bb7adea25","stem":"5 Acids, Bases and Buffers 87","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Acid","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"C","text":"it is acidic"},{"label":"D","text":"it is basic"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-53ed1e46a188","stem":"If a blood sample has a pH of 7.25, which of the following is NOT true?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the blood is acidic"},{"label":"B","text":"the person may be suffering from metabolic acidosis"},{"label":"C","text":"the person may be suffering from respiratory acidosis"},{"label":"D","text":"the blood is alkaline"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-04ead3a8f61b","stem":"Why does drinking acidic solutions such as orange juice NOT make the blood acidic?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blood is alkaline and alkaline solutions react with ingested acids to neutral- ise them."},{"label":"B","text":"The volume of water in the body is great enough that the effect of ingested acidic food and drink on pH is negligible."},{"label":"C","text":"The blood has buffers whose components are able to resist changes to blood pH."},{"label":"D","text":"The kidney is able to rapidly filter acidic components from blood to avoid pH changes."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-81e7b7d35699","stem":"Which statement about the phosphate buffer is correct?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Buffer","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"H2PO42− destroys acid and HPO4− destroys base"},{"label":"B","text":"H2PO42− destroys base and HPO4− destroys acid"},{"label":"C","text":"H2PO42− is basic and HPO4− is acidic"},{"label":"D","text":"the ratio of H2PO42− : HPO4− decreases following the addition of acid."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"739f1cc7a4f4b22bbd1211b9da10529fd55136b7","manualEdits":{"sourceStem":"Which statement about the phosphate buffer is correct?","sourceOptions":[{"label":"A","text":"H2PO42− destroys acid and HPO4− destroys base"},{"label":"B","text":"H2PO42− destroys base and HPO4− destroys acid"},{"label":"C","text":"H2PO42− is basic and HPO4− is acidic"},{"label":"D","text":"the ratio of H2PO42− : HPO4− decreases following the addition of acid. Answer is B: Di-hydrogen phosphate is able to destroy base by donating one of its hydrogen atoms (and in the process it becomes mono-hydrogen phosphate). Choice D is wrong as addition of acid would decrease the proportion of mono- hydrogen phosphate while increasing di-hydrogen phosphate. This would make the ratio increase, that is the “fraction” would be larger."}],"operations":["trailing_answer_leakage_removed"]}},{"id":"q-e36eff4c23f9","stem":"If the pH of a sample of blood was 7.4, which term below would be applied to it?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"acidotic"},{"label":"B","text":"acidic 88 4 Chemistry for Physiology"},{"label":"C","text":"alkalotic"},{"label":"D","text":"alkaline"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"19ffe4f2096a0309e486e76a3e7859106144b4fd"},{"id":"q-740ca680285d","stem":"Three of the solutions below have the SAME level of acidity (or alkalinity), the other is different. Which is the different one?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"blood"},{"label":"B","text":"a neutral solution"},{"label":"C","text":"a solution with pH = 7"},{"label":"D","text":"a solution with a hydrogen ion concentration of 10-7 mol/L"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-669130097b8e","stem":"Which of the following statements is a definition of a buffer solution?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Buffer","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"one where the number of osmoles of solute equals the number of moles."},{"label":"B","text":"one where the concentration of hydronium ions equals the concentration of hydroxide ions."},{"label":"C","text":"a solution that resists change to its pH."},{"label":"D","text":"a solution that reacts partly with water to produce a relatively low concen- tration of hydronium ions."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-26e2cc494910","stem":"Which of the following solutions is the most acidic?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"blood plasma"},{"label":"B","text":"gastric juice"},{"label":"C","text":"a solution with pH = 7"},{"label":"D","text":"a solution with hydronium ions at a concentration of 10-5 moles/L"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-17c2924a0357","stem":"Pure water contains small but equal amounts of hydronium (H3O+) ions and hydroxide (OH−) ions. This means that water is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"acidic"},{"label":"B","text":"basic"},{"label":"C","text":"acidic and basic"},{"label":"D","text":"neither acidic nor basic"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"b2d249a91b4bf5b1757f4bfe519a67d97b51d643"},{"id":"q-2b8581250702","stem":"What do we call a substance that when added to water, reacts with water to produce hydronium ions?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"an ionic compound"},{"label":"B","text":"a base"},{"label":"C","text":"an electrolyte"},{"label":"D","text":"an acid"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_no_source_key_bulk_v4","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-e509d6f209b6","stem":"How does the blood’s phosphate buffer behave when an hydronium ion is encountered?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Buffer","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"HPO42− destroys the hydronium ion and becomes H2PO4- – and blood pH decreases very slightly"},{"label":"B","text":"H2PO4- destroys the hydronium ion and becomes HPO42− and blood pH decreases very slightly"},{"label":"C","text":"HPO42− destroys the hydronium ion and becomes H2PO4- and blood pH increases very slightly"},{"label":"D","text":"H2PO4- destroys the hydronium ion and becomes HPO42− and blood pH increases very slightly"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"9ad0e6f15ed6b1b3b1d26945d93d2119aeb36840"},{"id":"q-5746f4acc6e6","stem":"One of the statements below about buffers is correct. Which one?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Buffer","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"a buffer consists of an acid and a base."},{"label":"B","text":"a buffer will react with water to produce hydronium ions."},{"label":"C","text":"a solution that is neither acidic nor basic is called a buffer."},{"label":"D","text":"the components of a buffer are conjugate pairs."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"independently_reasoned_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-2d79b12c932a","stem":"An acid may be defined as a substance that","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"reacts completely with water producing a high concentration of hydronium ions"},{"label":"B","text":"reacts with water to produce hydroxide ions"},{"label":"C","text":"produces a pH of more than 7 when in solution"},{"label":"D","text":"reacts with water to produce hydronium ions"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"fd382162282f8e634d9363ff1d7bb27ba3225f20"},{"id":"q-df20a57d1e0b","stem":"One of the statements below about buffers is WRONG. Which one?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Buffer","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"a buffer consists of a weak acid and its slightly basic salt."},{"label":"B","text":"a buffer will react with water to produce hydronium ions."},{"label":"C","text":"a solution that resists change to its pH is called a buffer."},{"label":"D","text":"the components of a buffer are conjugate pairs."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-3599d0511bb0","stem":"An acid may be defined by which one of the following statements?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"a substance with equal concentrations of hydronium and hydroxide ions."},{"label":"B","text":"a substance that reacts partly with water to produce hydronium ions"},{"label":"C","text":"a substance that maintains the pH of the solution it is in."},{"label":"D","text":"a substance that reacts with water producing hydroxide ions."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-bba90dce03d3","stem":"Which of the following defines an acid?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"when in solution, resists change to pH"},{"label":"B","text":"reacts with water to produce hydronium ions"},{"label":"C","text":"reacts with a buffer to produce water"},{"label":"D","text":"has a pH of more than 7."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-2e4d9a8502a2","stem":"Which of the following is NOT an acid?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"a solution having a pH of 3."},{"label":"B","text":"a substance which reacts with water to produce hydronium ions."},{"label":"C","text":"carbonic acid."},{"label":"D","text":"a solution that resists change to its pH"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_no_source_key_bulk_v4","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-4ce6092db16d","stem":"What effect would a base have when added to the following solutions?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tap water: – it would react to produce hydronium ions."},{"label":"B","text":"Tap water: – it would react to produce hydroxide ions."},{"label":"C","text":"Buffered water: – it would raise the pH of the solution."},{"label":"D","text":"Un-buffered water: – it would lower the pH of the solution."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-7a9e2565c0dd","stem":"What effect would an acid have when added to the following solutions?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tap water: – it would react to produce hydronium ions."},{"label":"B","text":"Tap water: – it would react to produce hydroxide ions."},{"label":"C","text":"Buffered water: – it would lower the pH of the solution."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"41c9bfb7b5366f2df504ce653c6d559cafd26fad"},{"id":"q-e9a4b6397ee1","stem":"A fresh sample of blood (a buffered solution) is withdrawn from the body and its pH measured at 7.4. A small quantity of strong acid (as an isotonic solution) is added to it. Which of the following values is most likely to be the pH of the resulting blood solution?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Buffer","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"3.0"},{"label":"B","text":"7.3"},{"label":"C","text":"7.4"},{"label":"D","text":"7.5"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{"sourceStem":"A fresh sample of blood (a buffered solution) is withdrawn from the body and its pH measured at 7.4. A small quantity of strong acid (as an isotonic solution) is added to it. Which of the following values is most likely to be the pH of the resulting blood solution?","sourceOptions":[{"label":"A","text":"3.0"},{"label":"B","text":"7.3"},{"label":"C","text":"7.4"},{"label":"D","text":"7.5 Answer is C: Blood is buffered so very little pH change will result from the addition of acid. Certainly not enough to change the pH by 0.1."}],"operations":["trailing_answer_leakage_removed"]}},{"id":"q-8a198d563d00","stem":"Blood in the body is different to a buffered solution held in a beaker. This is because hydronium (hydrogen) ions can be removed from solution in blood almost immediately by","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Buffer","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"filtration in the kidney from where it is eliminated from the body in urine."},{"label":"B","text":"combining with hydroxide ions to form water which is eliminated as urine."},{"label":"C","text":"combining with bicarbonate to form carbonic acid which disassociates to water and carbon dioxide which is breathed out."},{"label":"D","text":"reacting with mono-hydrogen phosphate to form di-hydrogen phosphate."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-c72f9ae25656","stem":"The definition of an acid is a substance that reacts","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"completely with water to produce a large concentration of hydronium ions."},{"label":"B","text":"with water to produce hydroxide ions."},{"label":"C","text":"with a base to produce a neutral salt."},{"label":"D","text":"with water to produce hydronium ions."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-debe4425a8c7","stem":"Choose the INCORRECT statement about buffer solutions.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Buffer","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the pH of a buffer does not change on addition of acid or base."},{"label":"B","text":"a buffer has two components which may be a weak acid and its salt."},{"label":"C","text":"a buffer has two components which may be a weak base and its salt."},{"label":"D","text":"pH of a buffer depends only on the ratio of its components."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"ce7002f300a72097fe1a58ddd09e91fdb9ec8dbb"},{"id":"q-d785a2df19ff","stem":"In the condition known as acidosis, the blood pH would be","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"greater than 7.45."},{"label":"B","text":"less than 7.45."},{"label":"C","text":"less than 7.35."},{"label":"D","text":"less than 7.00."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-0c114a4d5814","stem":"As the blood becomes more acidic, hydrogen (hydronium) ions combine with bicarbonate ions to form carbonic acid which in turn dissociates into carbon dioxide (to be breathed out by the lungs) and water. Which is the equation describing this reaction?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thorax","topicOriginal":"Thorax","subtopic":"Lung","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"CO 2 + H 2 O ← H 2 CO 3 ← H + + HCO 3 −"},{"label":"B","text":"CO 2 + H 2 O → H 2 CO 3 → H + + HCO 3 −"},{"label":"C","text":"CO 2 + H 2 O ← H + + HCO 3 − ← H 2 CO 3"},{"label":"D","text":"H + + HCO 3 − ← CO 2 + H 2 O ← H 2 CO 3"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_text"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thorax","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_text","prior_structural_review_flag","repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"f080e1b0bdd9ffc31b390cb174e7a2962e24b849"},{"id":"q-6e6aed5ee016","stem":"4, maintains the pH at 7.4. What is the ratio of these two components if the pH were to become 8.4 after the addition of more of the basic component of the buffer?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Ph","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"1:5"},{"label":"B","text":"2:4"},{"label":"C","text":"1:8"},{"label":"D","text":"1:40"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-6f4763301991","stem":"Which of the following pH values would you expect to a dilute solution of a weak base to have?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"2.4"},{"label":"B","text":"6.2"},{"label":"C","text":"8.2"},{"label":"D","text":"12.4"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-0c2567d0fb82","stem":"A buffer solution having a pH of 8.5 has a small amount of strong acid added to it. What is its pH value likely to be closest to?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Buffer","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-0f8cd490f714","stem":"6 Organic Chemistry and Macromolecules 93","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"5.0"},{"label":"B","text":"8.4"},{"label":"C","text":"8.6"},{"label":"D","text":"12.4"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-9a8b1267f965","stem":"Which of the following statements about the role of the kidneys or lungs in the maintenance of acid-base balance within the body is FALSE?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thorax","topicOriginal":"Thorax","subtopic":"Lung","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The kidneys are able to affect several buffer systems but the lungs affect only one."},{"label":"B","text":"The lungs exert their influence on the carbonic acid/bicarbonate buffer system."},{"label":"C","text":"Neither the lungs nor the kidneys have much influence over the protein buffer."},{"label":"D","text":"The kidneys are able to respond more rapidly than the lungs to changes in acid-­base balance."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thorax","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-2f44f0c20e91","stem":"Denaturation of proteins involves disruption of the","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"primary structure"},{"label":"B","text":"secondary structure"},{"label":"C","text":"tertiary structure"},{"label":"D","text":"covalent bonding"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-e95022d1c79c","stem":"One of the following is NOT a function of lipids in the body. Which one?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Lipids","topicOriginal":"Lipids","subtopic":"Lipid","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"function as co-enzymes."},{"label":"B","text":"used in phospholipids."},{"label":"C","text":"used to make prostaglandins."},{"label":"D","text":"steroids are produced from them."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Lipids","answerAuditStatus":"independently_reasoned_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-1c636dcf9a74","stem":"Amino acids are the building blocks of all proteins. As more amino acids are added the protein chain tends to fold upon itself in a characteristic way. This characteristic of a protein is called the","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Acid","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"primary structure"},{"label":"B","text":"secondary structure"},{"label":"C","text":"tertiary structure"},{"label":"D","text":"quaternary structure"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-91292a1dc4b9","stem":"Which of the following is the correct combination for the nucleic acid DNA?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosphate, Ribose, Uracil"},{"label":"B","text":"Phosphate, Deoxyribose, Uracil"},{"label":"C","text":"Phosphate, Ribose, Adenine"},{"label":"D","text":"Phosphate, Deoxyribose, Adenine"}],"priority":