duces the high- est yield of energy?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"100 gram of fat"},{"label":"B","text":"100 gram of chocolate"},{"label":"C","text":"100 gram of carbohydrate"},{"label":"D","text":"100 gram of protein 96 4 Chemistry for Physiology"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"22c270887994f23e05cff7a6a90d3e1f27be3699"},{"id":"q-9076e61267fe","stem":"Fructose is a simple sugar or carbohydrate. What is it an example of? A","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Carbohydrate Metabolism","topicOriginal":"Carbohydrate Metabolism","subtopic":"Carbohydrate","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"monosaccharide"},{"label":"B","text":"disaccharide"},{"label":"C","text":"polysaccharide"},{"label":"D","text":"oligosaccharide"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Carbohydrate Metabolism","answerAuditStatus":"independently_reasoned_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-f5f53c581ac3","stem":"Cells use glucose as an energy source, but store it as glycogen. When needed the glycogen is broken down by a process called","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subjec