ic; parasympathetic"},{"label":"B","text":"Cholinergic; sympathetic"},{"label":"C","text":"Adrenergic; parasympathetic"},{"label":"D","text":"Adrenergic; sympathetic"},{"label":"E","text":"Cholinergic; motor"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Neurology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"52a613a75d258f44a77de1c4209ddef802757f87"},{"id":"q-90192bb1c75d","stem":"Which of the following disease is associated with joints?a) Goutb) Osteoporosisc) Arthritisd) TetanyView Answer Explanation: Arthritis is inflammation and painful joints due to degradation of cartilages or synovial membrane or deposition of uric acid. advertisement","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a4ca93f9e905","stem":"Which cartilage is present at the joints of long bones?a) Calcifiedb) Elasticc) Hyalined) FibrousView Answer Explanation: Hyaline is present at the joints of long bones.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7f138e14bf70","stem":"Which type of joint lies at the shoulder joint and hip joint?a) Pivot jointb) Angular jointc) Ball and socket jointd) Hinge jointView Answer Explanation: Ball and socket joints have a ball like spherical end called head which fits into cup like depression. It is freely movable in all directions.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Head","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8f869c8afe13","stem":"Which of the following pair is correctly matched?a) Cartilaginous joint- skull bonesb) Gliding joint- between zygapophyses of vertebraec) Hinge joint- between vertebraed) Fibrous joint- between phalangesView Answer Explanation: Zygapophyses are projections of the vertebrae that serve the purpose of fitting with an adjacent vertebra, the actual region of contact is called articular facet.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Face","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-66226d727d81","stem":"A young man is brought to the hospital after sustaining injury to the right hip in motor traffic accident. The patient is conscious with normal vital sign. Local examination revealed that the right hip is flexed, externally rotated abducted and the limb is elongated. The most likely diagnosis of this patient is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Histology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Central hip dislocation"},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior hip dislocation"},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior hip dislocation"},{"label":"D","text":"Closed fracture of femur"},{"label":"E","text":"Fracture dislocation of the femur Answerr is B"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Histology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"53ea46401fda9b3228a0dde0774798121d3a212f"},{"id":"q-d462f37b7429","stem":"Which of the following is an example of syndesmoses?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"coronal suture B. distal tibiofibular joint"},{"label":"C","text":"hip joint D. intercarpal joints B"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6a133c03f6c5","stem":"Which of the following movements are possible in pivot joint?","type":"Singl