 the answer which is the most appropriate to the term “receptor”","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"All types of ion channels modulated by a drug"},{"label":"B","text":"Enzymes of oxidizing-reducing reactions activated by a drug"},{"label":"C","text":"Active macromolecular components of a cell or an organism which a drug molecule has to combine with in order to elicit its specific effect"},{"label":"D","text":"Carriers activated by a drug"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"newly_reasoned_provisional","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f53d487f2a39","stem":"What does “affinity” mean?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A measure of how tightly a drug binds to plasma proteins"},{"label":"B","text":"A measure of how tightly a drug binds to a receptor"},{"label":"C","text":"A measure of inhibiting potency of a drug"},{"label":"D","text":"A measure of bioavailability of a drug"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independently_reasoned_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-eff32d134e14","stem":"Target proteins which a drug molecule binds are","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Only receptors"},{"label":"B","text":"Only ion channels"},{"label":"C","text":"Only carriers 9"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independently_reasoned_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"e73f167ad5a8f5435cc6c5eb8660c950b3f76c66"},{"id":"q-517467a8e120","stem":"An agonist is a substance that","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interacts with the receptor without producing any effect"},{"label":"B","text":"Interacts with the receptor and initiates changes in cell function, producing various effects"},{"label":"C","text":"Increases concentration of another substance to produce effect"},{"label":"D","text":"Interacts with plasma proteins and doesn’t produce any effect"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independently_reasoned_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-715246756b97","stem":"If an agonist can produce maximal effects and has high efficacy it’s called","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Partial agonist"},{"label":"B","text":"Antagonist"},{"label":"C","text":"Agonist-antagonist"},{"label":"D","text":"Full agonist"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independently_reasoned_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4ebd4f709785","stem":"If an agonist can produce submaximal effects and has moderate efficacy it’s called","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Partial agonist"},{"label":"B","text":"Antagonist"},{"label":"C","text":"Agonist-antagonist"},{"label":"D","text":"Full agonist"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independently_reasoned_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7dd75778404e","stem":"An antagonist is a substance that","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Binds to the receptors and initiates changes in cell function, producing maximal effect"},{"label":"B","text":"Binds to the receptors and initiates changes in cell function, producing submaximal effect"},{"label":"C","text":"Interacts with plasma proteins and doesn’t produce any effect"},{"label":"D","text":"Binds to the receptors without directly altering their functions"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independently_reasoned_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e61ff8b020a0","stem":"A competitive antagonist is a substance that","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interacts with receptors and produces submaximal effect"},{"label":"B","text":"Binds to the same receptor site and progressively inhibits the agonist response"},{"label":"C","text":"Binds to the nonspecific sites of tissue"},{"label":"D","text":"Binds to one receptor subtype as an agonist and to another as an antagonist"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independently_reasoned_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f73749cbd668","stem":"The substance binding to one receptor subtype as an agonist and to another as an antagonist is called","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Competitive antagonist"},{"label":"B","text":"Irreversible antagonist"},{"label":"C","text":"Agonist-antagonist"},{"label":"D","text":"Partial agonist"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"independent_no_source_key_bulk_v4","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ccb0f3370dbe","stem":"Irreversible interaction of an antagonist with a receptor is due to","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ionic bonds"},{"label":"B","text":"Hydrogen bonds"},{"label":"C","text":"Covalent bonds"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"independently_reasoned_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9727f1a2cfe1","stem":"Mechanisms of transmembrane signaling are the following EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transmembrane receptors that bind and stimulate a protein tyrosine kinase"},{"label":"B","text":"Gene replacement by the introduction of a therapeutic gene to correct a genetic effect"},{"label":"C","text":"Ligand-gated ion channels that can be induced to open or close by binding a ligand"},{"label":"D","text":"Transmembrane receptor protein that stimulates a GTP-binding signal transducer protein (G-protein) which in turn generates an intracellular second messenger"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"newly_reasoned_provisional","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f670061381eb","stem":"Tick the second messenger of G-protein-coupled (metabotropic) receptor","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenylyl cyclase"},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium ions"},{"label":"C","text":"Phospholipase C"},{"label":"D","text":"cAMP"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"independently_reasoned_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a659d775a8cb","stem":"Tick the substance which changes the activity of an effector element but doesn’t belong to second messengers","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"cAMP"},{"label":"B","text":"cGMP"},{"label":"C","text":"G–protein"},{"label":"D","text":"Calcium ions"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_no_source_key_bulk_v4","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-eb7d01d69277","stem":"The increase of second messengers’ (cAMP, cGMP, Ca2+ etc.) concentration leads to","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of intracellular protein kinases and protein phosphorylation"},{"label":"B","